---
description: Production-grade Next.js engineering standards
alwaysApply: true
---

- Prefer Server Components by default.
- Only use `"use client"` when interactivity is required.
- Avoid unnecessary client-side state and effects.
- Fetch data on the server whenever possible.
- Keep components small and composable.
- Separate UI, business logic, and data access.
- Never expose secrets or private environment variables to the client.
- Use `NEXT_PUBLIC_` only for intentionally public variables.
- Validate all external input.
- Handle loading, empty, and error states explicitly.
- Add proper TypeScript types. Avoid `any`.
- Prefer async/await over promise chains.
- Avoid deeply nested components and props drilling.
- Use dynamic imports for heavy client components.
- Optimize images with Next.js Image component.
- Prefer route handlers or server actions for backend logic.
- Avoid duplicate API calls and unnecessary re-renders.
- Keep API responses typed and predictable.
- Write accessible HTML and semantic components.
- Prefer Tailwind utility classes over custom CSS when possible.
- Avoid inline anonymous functions in heavily rendered lists.
- Use descriptive naming for files, components, and functions.
- Remove dead code, console logs, and commented code before completion.
- Keep dependencies minimal and avoid unnecessary packages.
- Follow existing project architecture and conventions.
- Prioritize readability and maintainability over clever abstractions.